\chapter{MuLLIER: from syntax to execution}
MuLLIER is MuLL's intermediate representation and pass pipeline. Parsing still
uses the lexer and precedence parser in \api{MuDSL.hpp} and \api{MuDSL.d}.
Each successful parse now follows the same sequence in both hosts:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Lower the syntax tree to MuDSL \api{SExpr} values.
\item Analyze lexical declarations, reference targets, and loop control.
\item Saturate an e-graph with transformation rules and extract a compact term.
\item Raise that term to the executable tree, retaining source locations.
\item Evaluate using the existing cells, environments, callbacks, and budgets.
\end{enumerate}
The same path handles scripts and package initializers. A cached package does
not initialize again. Host callbacks call captured executable functions directly.

\section{The interchange contract}
Every node is a list containing its operator, quoted payload, name list, source
site, and ordered children. Empty metadata lists retain their literal heads.
This makes all terms suitable for MuDSL's e-graph, whose lists require atom heads.
\begin{rulecode}
(binary "and" (names) (site 1 13)
  (literal "true" (names) (site 1 8))
  (number "42" (names) (site 1 17)))
\end{rulecode}
The payload is always quoted: a string such as \api{?x} must never become a
pattern variable. A function's \api{names} list contains parameters; a record's
contains keys in evaluation order. \api{site} stores a one-based line and byte
column. The outer execution boundary attaches the source filename to diagnostics.
\api{dump}, \api{parse_sexpr}, \api{encode_sexpr}, and \api{decode_sexpr}
provide text and binary interchange using MuDSL itself.

\section{Semantic analysis before effects}
Same-scope duplicates, assignments to statically known immutable bindings,
invalid reference targets, and \api{break} outside a loop are diagnosed before
execution. A function resets the loop context: its break cannot escape the caller.
Analysis includes unreachable branches, so malformed control flow is rejected
even when a later optimization would remove the branch.

MuLL closures retain live environments. A name can be supplied by the host or
declared later in a captured scope. Analysis therefore leaves unresolved names
and captured-cell mutability to the evaluator; it is not a static type checker.
This distinction preserves recursion and later declarations. Runtime type,
bounds, import, and arithmetic failures still occur at their evaluation points.

\section{Inspecting a compilation}
\begin{cppcode}
mull::Runtime vm;
auto answer = vm.run("return true and 42;", "answer.mull");
assert(answer.number() == 42);
std::cout << vm.mullier.lastIR.dump() << '\n';
vm.mullier.optimize = false;
\end{cppcode}
Disabling optimization leaves semantic analysis active. The IR and saturation
report describe the most recently compiled unit, which may be an imported
package. Independent runtimes have independent pass settings and rule sets.
